From 753e731dbc527dc99927b55d6aa21286987fd408 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: vshulcz <vshulcz@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 10:10:50 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] cmd/go: make go clean -cache wait for go commands using the
 cache

go clean -cache removes entries that a running build has already looked
up, and the compiler, linker or vet then fails with "no such file or
directory". Go commands that use the build cache now hold a shared lock
on GOCACHE/clean.lock, and go clean -cache takes an exclusive lock
before removing anything. go run, go tool and go doc -http release the
lock before starting the program, except on Windows, where the program
may be running from the cache.

TODO(numbers): quiet-machine re-measure. Four loops of go build ./...
and go vet ./... on a 56-package module with go clean -cache started
every 2s: N of M runs failed on a missing cache file before (C cleans),
N of M with this change (C cleans, clean wait p50/max). Lock cost per
go command and warm go build/vet/list times (n=20).

A clean waits until no go command is using the cache and says so after
a second. TODO(numbers): wait behind K back-to-back build loops. The
second lock suggested in the issue would shorten that wait, but a go
command started without GOCACHE_INTERNAL_LOCKED by a test or a
-toolexec wrapper would then deadlock with its parent and the clean.

Programs run by a go command that holds the lock get the locked
directory in GOCACHE_INTERNAL_LOCKED. A go command they start does not
lock that cache again, so go clean -cache run from a test does not wait
for its own go test. Such a clean can still break the parent, as before.

Fixes #31948

Change-Id: Ie8d90299491beb0625824fb465e058ca48b334d0
